<Page
  title="Methodology"
  eyebrow="Editorial"
  lede="How a profile on this site is researched, written and checked."
  updated="29 August 2026"
>
  <h2>1. Establish the entity</h2>
  <p>
    Research starts by fixing the subject's identity: exact name, location, and any identifier that distinguishes it
    from similarly named entities — a registration number, a school code, an address. Where two entities share a name,
    that ambiguity is stated in the profile rather than resolved silently.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Search the record, not the summaries</h2>
  <p>
    We look for primary documentation first: government registries, regulator lists, awarding-body publications, court
    and corporate filings. Press coverage is then searched across the full period the entity has existed, not only
    recent results.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Read every source</h2>
  <p>
    Each candidate source is opened and read against the specific claim it would support. A source is only cited if it
    contains that claim. This step is not optional and is not delegated to a search snippet or an AI summary; research
    assistance may propose sources, but a proposed source is treated as a lead until a human has read the document.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Separate the kinds of evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Claims are sorted into independent reporting, institutional records, government records and first-party
    statements. The profile keeps them visibly distinct. Where a source is used only to establish that something
    exists — an award programme, say — rather than that the subject received it, the profile says so.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Record what failed</h2>
  <p>
    Claims that could not be verified are listed in an "Open items" section on the profile. A claim that appeared in a
    draft and did not survive checking is removed, not softened with hedging language. Nothing is published on the
    strength of a source we could not open.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Write to a fixed template</h2>
  <p>
    Each entity type has its own template — a school profile and a person profile ask different questions — so that
    profiles are comparable and a reader knows where to look. Structured data is emitted for the entity type, using
    only fields for which the profile holds evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Date it and keep it</h2>
  <p>
    Every profile carries a publication date, a last-updated date and a named author. Profiles are revisited when new
    documentation appears, and updates are reflected in the last-updated date.
  </p>
</Page>
